From e8fd925967d828d2fce41b9e69e220b6dc2da09e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 01:09:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer, utils: give the bridge each picture's HDR10+
 metadata for Dolby Vision Low-Latency, and say it in the log

A picture of HDR10+ sent as Dolby Vision that is drawn natively (a VAAPI
surface, or the planes of a picture that the processor decoded) now takes its
own HDR10+ metadata to the bridge (CDVBridgeGLES::Layer::hdr10plus), as the
decoder holds them for it (CMetadataHold: its own, or those of the last one
over fewer than 12 pictures without) and as the bridge maps them
(dvbridge_hdr10plus_plain()). They are set on the renderer with every picture
that is prepared, made ready first or checked
(dvbridge_render_set_hdr10plus()), so that a picture is always mapped by its
own; in Low-Latency the bridge maps it by their tone curve. Any other picture,
Dolby Vision of its own with HDR10+ beside it among them, takes none
(KODI::UTILS::HDR10PLUS::ForDolbyVision()) and is drawn as before.

kodi.log says it, once a video: the renderer's line on the tone curve says, in
Low-Latency, that Hades maps the picture by this curve without leaving Dolby
Vision (or why the curve is not used, or that level 1 maps a video whose
metadata carry none), and the bridge's line says how (the target, the knee
point, the anchors, the display's peak and how libplacebo adapts the curve to
it), "inside Dolby Vision Low-Latency". A GPU that cannot draw the curve is
said once, and its pictures are mapped from level 1. The line for Dolby Vision
Standard says that in Low-Latency Hades maps the picture by the curve.

The frame presented says whether it was mapped so
(CDVBridgeGLES::PresentedDetail::hdr10PlusCurve). Checked on the host, against
xbmc/utils/HDR10PlusVsif.h itself: which metadata a picture takes to the
bridge, and the line for Low-Latency and for Standard, 11 checks, each of five
mutants caught; the checks of the hold and of the other lines pass as before
(20).
